diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..28a2cee
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,78 @@
+# Chat bots
+
+From **Account settings → Set up chat bots**, choose **Add a chat bot**. Give it a name and, optionally, an avatar. After saving, copy its **Bot code**. Members can manage the bots they create; administrators can manage all bots.
+
+Administrators can set **Bots per member** in account settings. The default is five. Deleted bots don’t count, and administrators can create unlimited bots. Lowering the limit keeps existing bots; members can add another once they’re below the limit.
+
+Bots don’t join rooms by default. Use **Choose open rooms** on the bot form to pick individual open rooms. Room administrators can also add a bot by selecting its name in an open or closed room’s settings. To have a bot serve every open room instead, turn on **Add to all open rooms** when creating or editing the bot. This includes existing rooms and any that become open later. Turning it off keeps current memberships.
+
+## Posting a message
+
+Use the numeric room ID from the room URL. Store `BOT_CODE` in your CI secret store and send the message as the request body (UTF-8 text, not a form field or JSON object):
+
+```sh
+curl --fail --data-binary 'Deploy of v1.2.3 finished ✅' \
+  "https://chat.example.com/rooms/${ROOM_ID}/${BOT_CODE}/messages"
+```
+
+`--data-binary @filename` also works for multiline text. If using an existing Active Storage upload, you can supply its signed blob ID in the `attachment` query parameter instead of, or in addition to, text.
+
+Success returns `201 Created` and a `Location` header pointing to `/messages/:id`. An invalid or deleted bot code returns `401`, an unknown or inaccessible room returns `404`, and a request without text or an attachment returns `422`.
+
+## Receiving webhooks
+
+Set **Webhook URL** when creating or editing a bot. Use a publicly reachable HTTP(S) endpoint you control; use HTTPS to protect the room content and posting credential in transit. Clear the field and save to disable callbacks.
+
+### Mentions
+
+When a person selects your bot from the `@` suggestions and posts the message, Campfire sends a background `POST` with `Content-Type: application/json`. A bot gets one callback per message, even if mentioned more than once. For example:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "mention",
+  "author": { "id": 7, "name": "Alice" },
+  "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/<bot-code>/messages" },
+  "message": {
+    "id": 118,
+    "body": {
+      "text": "restart the build please",
+      "html": "<div>… full rich-text body, including the mention …</div>"
+    },
+    "link": "/rooms/5/@118"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+`message.body.text` omits this bot’s mentions, but retains mentions of other room members. `message.link` and `room.reply_to` are paths relative to your Campfire origin.
+
+### Room joins
+
+When the bot joins an open or closed room, whether automatically or by invitation, Campfire sends a `joined` event to the same endpoint. The bot is notified once per room. Direct conversations never trigger this event.
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "joined",
+  "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/<bot-code>/messages" }
+}
+```
+
+### Responding to either event
+
+- To answer now, return `200` and a UTF-8 `text/plain` or `text/html` body. Campfire posts the reply as the bot; plain text is posted literally, and HTML is sanitized.
+- To answer later (or not at all), return `204`. When ready, POST plain text to `room.reply_to` as described above. **Do not log or share this path**: it contains the bot code.
+
+Campfire does not follow webhook redirects. Failed requests, unsupported response types, and empty or oversized replies (over 1 MiB) do not create messages. Bot-authored messages do not trigger further webhooks.
+
+## Automatic suspension
+
+Each bot can post up to 10 messages in a rolling 10-second window across all its rooms. API posts and immediate webhook replies share this limit. The next message is rejected and starts a 30-second cooldown. The API returns `429 Too Many Requests` with `Retry-After` set to the remaining seconds. Wait until it expires: retries don’t extend the cooldown, but five more attempts during that same cooldown suspend the bot. Replies returned by a webhook during cooldown also count as attempts and are discarded.
+
+Ten consecutive failed webhook deliveries (including non-`2xx` responses, timeouts, or connection errors) also suspend a bot. Any `2xx` response resets the streak, even if it contains no usable reply.
+
+A suspended bot receives no webhooks, and its posting URL returns `403 Forbidden`. It keeps its code, memberships and messages, and still counts toward its owner’s limit. The bots page shows **Suspended** and the reason. After fixing the bot, ask an administrator to choose **Reactivate** and confirm. This clears the cooldown and counters. These safeguards are not configurable.
+
+## Keeping the code private
+
+Treat the entire posting URL like a password. Use HTTPS and keep it out of repositories, shell tracing, and CI logs. Campfire redacts the credential from its Rails request logs. If you run a reverse proxy or collect request URLs elsewhere, omit or redact these paths there too. Keep Thruster’s unfiltered request logging disabled (`THRUSTER_LOG_REQUESTS=false` in the Docker image).
+
+Deleting the bot revokes its code without removing its previous messages. If a code has been exposed, delete the bot, create a replacement, and update the secret in your scripts.
diff --git a/lib/middleware/bot_message_request.rb b/lib/middleware/bot_message_request.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..70c6114
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/middleware/bot_message_request.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+# The public bot URL contains a bearer credential. Extract it before Rails logs
+# the path; parameter filtering alone only handles the query string and params.
+class BotMessageRequest
+  PATH = %r{\A(/rooms/[^/]+/)([^/]+)(/messages/?)\z}
+
+  def initialize(app)
+    @app = app
+  end
+
+  def call(env)
+    if match = PATH.match(env["PATH_INFO"])
+      env["campfire.bot_code"] = match[2]
+      env["PATH_INFO"] = "#{match[1]}redacted#{match[3]}"
+      %w[ REQUEST_URI REQUEST_PATH ORIGINAL_FULLPATH ].each do |key|
+        env[key] = env[key].sub(match[0], env["PATH_INFO"]) if env[key]
+      end
+
+      # Even curl -d (form content type) sends literal text here. Don't let
+      # Rack::MethodOverride or Rails parse the body as parameters.
+      env["CONTENT_TYPE"] = "text/plain"
+    end
+
+    @app.call(env)
+  end
+end
